VisualOn AAC encoder library
============================

This library contains an encoder implementation of the Advanced Audio
Coding (AAC) audio codec. The library is based on a codec implementation
by VisualOn as part of the Stagefright framework from the Google Android
project.

Optional AAC-LC PNS
-------------------

`VO_PID_AAC_PNS` (`VO_PID_AAC_Mdoule | 0x0042`) enables optional Perceptual
Noise Substitution for 44.1/48 kHz, 1024-sample mono and ordinary stereo
streams. The parameter is a pointer to `VO_S32`:

* `0`: disable PNS (the default, preserving the original bitstream);
* `1`: enable PNS.

`SetParam` returns `VO_ERR_INVALID_ARG` for a null pointer or any value other
than 0 or 1 and leaves the last legal request unchanged. `GetParam` returns
the currently effective value. A legal change is pending until the next
complete PCM frame actually starts encoding; a partial frame supplied through
the buffer API does not make the change effective early. Changing the switch
does not reopen the encoder core or reset MDCT overlap, window-switching
state, cached PCM, or bit-reservoir state.

PNS candidates are chosen from the physical MDCT spectrum after the transform
and before TNS/MS processing. A physical scalefactor band must start at or
above 4 kHz, contain at least eight lines, have positive energy, and satisfy
`n * max(x*x) <= 8 * sum(x*x)` using overflow-safe integer arithmetic. All
physical windows in a short-window group must pass. PNS bands are written with
section codebook 13, the 9-bit initial noise preamble and the independent AAC
noise differential scalefactor chain. When PNS is active, TNS is not used; MS
continues only on ordinary bands and is disabled for a band where either side
uses PNS.

PNS is most useful for low-bitrate recordings with steady high-frequency
noise, tape/hiss, or noisy live recordings. It spends fewer bits reproducing
unpredictable high-frequency components and can make such material sound
smoother and less "warbly". Tonal high-frequency content and peak-like bands
remain ordinary spectral coding. At high bitrates or for bright, detailed
material, PNS can reduce air/detail or make cymbals and fricatives sound
slightly more synthetic; A/B listening at the target bitrate is recommended.
Leave it off for maximum transparency when the reservoir already has enough
bits for the high bands.

The C example `examples/pns_adts.c` writes a continuous sequence of ADTS
frames with PNS enabled:

```sh
bash task_support/build.sh
cc -std=c99 -O2 -I. -Iaacenc/inc -Icommon/include \
   examples/pns_adts.c .task-build/libcodec.a -lm -o pns_adts
./pns_adts pns_adts.aac 100 1
```

Raw AAC is selected by setting `AACENC_PARAM.adtsUsed` to 0; the PNS parameter
and frame rules are otherwise the same.

Tests
-----

`tests/pns_encode.c` registers `selection`, `syntax`, and `streaming` cases
through `task_support/check.h`. Run:

```sh
bash task_support/run.sh target
```

The tests generate fixed broadband-noise, narrow-band-tone and transient
signals, parse codebooks/sections/both scalefactor chains/spectral boundaries
from real AAC packets, and compare those results with read-only spectrum
observations. They also check whole-frame versus irregular even-byte chunk
input, interleaved instances, parameter timing/errors, and default-off byte
compatibility. `bash task_support/run.sh baseline` encodes the supplied fixed
PCM baseline.
